/**
\fn Part& pdg::Part::setIKLimits(PhysicsConstraint& constraint)
\brief Share a live physical rotary limit with this IK joint.

Retains the constraint and reads its current interval on each solve and limit
query. Changing PhysicsConstraint::setAngleLimits() changes the physical limit
and the IK range together. This replaces any numeric or linked IK limits without
changing the pose, creating bodies, or creating joints.

The active constraint must connect this Part's body to its immediate parent
Part's body, or to the owning Sprite's body for a root Part. Either endpoint
order is accepted: reversed endpoints negate and exchange the bounds, so IK
queries still report this Part's rotation relative to its parent.

The parent frame must be uniform, unreflected, and aligned with the parent body's
orientation. This Part must have positive effective scale axes. Authored source
frames on a root Part and imported physical-rig bodies are unsupported. The
interval must fit Part rotations and span at most one turn. Invalid setup leaves
the old configuration intact. These conditions are checked again when used.

If the constraint breaks, disconnects, changes to an unsupported interval, or no
longer connects the correct bodies, direct solves and angle queries throw.
Persistent IK records getIKError() and holds its pose; physical IK stops all its
owned drives. The link remains configured, so hasIKLimits() stays true. Clear or
replace it to recover; temporary frame/range errors recover when corrected.
Clearing the link does not disconnect the physical constraint. Its retained
handle stays safe after body removal.

\param constraint An active PhysicsConstraint of type constraint_RotaryLimit.
\return This Part for chaining.

\note Initial Sprite/Layer snapshots retain the live constraint link. Save the
containing Layer if its constraint endpoints span Sprites. Disconnected links and
invalid live configurations must be cleared or repaired before saving. Numeric
limits also round-trip without physics.

\see PhysicsConstraint::setAngleLimits(), clearIKLimits(), setIKDriveTarget()
*/

/**
\fn Part& pdg::Part::setIKLimits(double minAngle, double maxAngle)
\brief Limit this joint's local rotation when solving Part IK.

Angles are radians in the Part's parent frame. Both bounds must be finite,
representable as Part rotations, and
ordered, spanning at most one full turn. Equal bounds lock the desired angle.
The interval may cross a multiple of a full turn by using unwrapped values, such
as 6.0 through 6.5. Invalid input leaves the old limits intact. This configuration
does not immediately rotate the Part or constrain ordinary Animated calls.

Set limits on the root and/or middle Part. Direct and persistent IK honor them;
physical IK limits its desired pose. Physical bodies can overshoot or be pushed
outside that pose: use PhysicsBody::createRotaryLimit() for a physical constraint.
When both bend solutions satisfy the limits, bendDirection selects the preferred
one. Otherwise the solve chooses the nearest permitted endpoint in the chain's
parent coordinate frame. Positive partial influence remains inside the allowed interval.
Numeric limits replace any live constraint link and are included in ordinary Part snapshots in both resource modes. Use the PhysicsConstraint overload to share an existing physical limit.

\param minAngle Minimum local rotation, in radians.
\param maxAngle Maximum local rotation, in radians.
\return This Part for chaining.
\see clearIKLimits(), solveIK(), setIKDriveTarget()
*/

/**
\fn Part& pdg::Part::clearIKLimits()
\brief Remove this joint's IK rotation limits.

\return This Part for chaining. Does not change the current pose or disconnect a linked physical constraint.
*/

/**
\fn bool pdg::Part::hasIKLimits() const
\brief Check whether this joint has IK rotation limits.

\return True when setIKLimits() has configured numeric limits or a constraint link, including an invalid or disconnected link.
*/

/**
\fn double pdg::Part::getIKMinAngle() const
\brief Read the minimum local IK rotation.

\return The lower bound in local radians, or zero without limits. A linked constraint is read live; an invalid link throws.
*/

/**
\fn double pdg::Part::getIKMaxAngle() const
\brief Read the maximum local IK rotation.

\return The upper bound in local radians, or zero without limits. A linked constraint is read live; an invalid link throws.
*/

/**
\fn bool pdg::Part::isIKDriven() const
\brief Check whether this Part owns a physical IK controller.

\return True for a configured physical IK controller, including while it holds a structural error; false for direct IK or no controller.
*/

/**
\fn Part& pdg::Part::setIKDriveTarget(Part* middle, Part* tip, const Point& target, double maxForce, double maxTorque, int space, int bendDirection, double influence, double frequency, double dampingRatio)
\brief Drive a two-segment Part chain toward an IK target using bounded forces and torques.

Installs a persistent controller. Each step computes a desired pose after Part
animation and before the physics solve. The solver retains authority over all
physical positions and rotations; registering or updating a target does not
teleport the chain. The root and middle must have independent dynamic bodies.
The tip may have a dynamic body or no body. All three Parts must form a contiguous
chain in one Sprite. Imported physical-rig Parts are not supported by this controller.

The first assignment captures the root's parent-relative anchor and the middle
and tip joint offsets. Repeated assignments to the same chain retain those values,
so physical drift cannot redefine segment lengths. Clear and reinstall to capture
a new chain configuration. Ancestor movement and the current Part scales still
apply. The root requires uniform absolute scale, as with solveIK().

The controller owns each participating body drive until clearIKTarget(). Existing
independent body drives or overlapping IK controllers cause setup to throw without
replacing the old controller. Direct setDriveTarget(), clearDrive(), stopAllForces()
and changes away from dynamic mode reject while the controller owns the body.
Impulses and other forces may still disturb it. No joints are created implicitly:
configure pivot joints, physical angular limits and collision shapes separately.
Any pivot or pin between adjacent driven Parts must join the captured parent
offset to the child origin (pin distance zero). Mismatched anchors reject setup
or suspend a running controller. Anchors are unscaled body-local coordinates;
account for Part scaling when configuring them.

The root's target position is its captured anchor; the middle and physical tip
receive the solved joint positions. Body orientations use the shortest rotation
route, except joints sharing a rotary limit preserve its unwrapped relative
angle so the drive does not request a shortcut through the physical stop. Force/torque caps apply separately to each body. A physical tip retains its
captured local orientation relative to the desired middle pose. Influence zero
turns off the controller's drive contribution without releasing ownership.

A missing/replaced body or invalid topology stops all this controller's drives
and records getIKError(). Valid topology can resume; after replacing a body,
call setIKDriveTarget() again to claim it. isIKTargetReached() measures the current
physical endpoint, so a constrained or force-limited chain may remain unreached
without a configuration error. Clearing preserves velocities, constraints and
independently applied loads.

\param middle This Part's immediate child, with a dynamic PhysicsBody.
\param tip Middle's immediate child, with a dynamic body or no body.
\param target Desired endpoint in the selected coordinate space.
\param maxForce Finite nonnegative force cap per driven body.
\param maxTorque Finite nonnegative torque cap per driven body.
\param space partSpace_World by default; partSpace_Sprite or partSpace_Local (root's parent frame) are also accepted.
\param bendDirection +1 by default, or -1 for the other bend direction.
\param influence Pose influence from zero through one; default 1.
\param frequency Positive drive frequency in cycles per second; default 4.
\param dampingRatio Nonnegative drive damping ratio; default 1.
\return This Part for chaining.

\note Advance the owning SpriteLayer normally. Bodies use either the basic or
Chipmunk solver; step size, constraints and force caps affect convergence.
Initial Sprite/Layer snapshots preserve the controller, captured joint offsets,
body drives and shared rotary limits in either resource mode. Save the containing
Layer when a joint or compound collider references another Sprite. Restored
controllers retain exclusive ownership of their bodies and resume on the next step.

\par Example
\code{.cpp}
auto* shoulder = sprite.createPart("shoulder");
auto* elbow = sprite.createPart("elbow");
auto* wrist = sprite.createPart("wrist");
elbow->setParentPart(shoulder).setLocation(10, 0);
wrist->setParentPart(elbow).setLocation(8, 0);
shoulder->setupPhysicsBody(1, 20);
elbow->setupPhysicsBody(1, 20);
shoulder->physics.createPivotJoint(elbow->physics, pdg::Point(10, 0));
auto& elbowLimit = shoulder->physics.createRotaryLimit(elbow->physics, 0, 2.5);
elbow->setIKLimits(elbowLimit);
shoulder->setIKDriveTarget(elbow, wrist, pdg::Point(10, 8), 100, 40,
                          pdg::partSpace_Sprite);
\endcode

\see setIKTarget(), setIKLimits(), clearIKTarget(), PhysicsBody::setDriveTarget()
*/
